 the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/cdca164d343d8bff4c78a1e366e52c588ca6c54dfdea61acea1900bcbdc79f31.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and II are false. As we have seen, $A$ contains all its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161625/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161625"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161622/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "b01d0659-cb8d-569e-9eae-9ff8c0d2ec82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161622/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b743fa62-6f63-567c-b90c-2ed0391e9e53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161622/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "0291649b-609c-5efe-afd8-fc4e43c8ac62" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161622/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7c3aa9cc-38a0-5be5-ad04-eedb6acb478e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161622/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5d279c7d-b657-5646-9c60-59c9c5a567c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161622/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "927d3a65-73f1-55ef-906d-e986eb1d878c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161622/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-161622/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-161622/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-161622/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161622/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-161622/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161622" :question/id #uuid "157d4457-1f9d-56e1-8728-9f512b3e63e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161622" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1+x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/3e7d8c11ac4b3de8473741a362ad2b78555c764da2d61f955435e8d59771cf46.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains all of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains all of its boundary points, we must have $A-∂A⊊A.$ Therefore $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161622/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161622"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161626/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "b1e2527a-df6e-53b9-8a50-db65ef333ac6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161626/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c661fcfe-c80e-57aa-985a-513b1f79f913" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161626/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "de93781e-a6ed-5713-96bf-ffa75c19b73b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161626/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b998510f-38fa-54a4-9474-b1cec36cded4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161626/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e87ff6e8-d276-588e-8bfd-078bb9d41f3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161626/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "01680378-49dc-5671-b5de-e62d50e41efd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161626/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-161626/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-161626/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-161626/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-161626/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161626/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161626" :question/id #uuid "81147b8a-c890-53be-ba74-33cc71183412" :question/math-academy-id "q-161626" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}<4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∂A$\n- $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/4540e03fc5404474a0cd900c89004e6a5cfe7ac2d4702348e9d0395fc1c914e9.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/2a93b912f0d7b3a84bf0ec9c3b3596d080a9e71a345ff436e2f591edf042d9db.png)\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $A=\\text{int}\\,A$ or, equivalently,  $A-\\text{int}\\,A=∅.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂{R}^{2}-A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161626/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161626"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161643/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "1b89315a-66ee-5cb3-bf1a-b190c5bfc2c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161643/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "90507c41-1430-51e3-9bbc-9e666ac8cbdb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161643/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "86f324ac-8393-5a4e-b025-26403f65a974" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161643/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "55be89ac-4e7e-510a-9264-714c7968d02e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161643/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2efcda97-048f-51b6-a815-2d871192a1a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161643/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0470d837-42ad-533e-912b-a4e9efc38a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161643/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-161643/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-161643/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161643/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-161643/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-161643/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161643" :question/id #uuid "d7f2a33f-92c6-54d6-a86e-fbe7b1778994" :question/math-academy-id "q-161643" :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-161643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a024c46e-0374-5d03-b895-eb754ff7ad97"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-161444"] :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/62c410c21e79e1bc688c1ff1a3440a207c62bcad42525bdc93fa2770b76d48bd.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/33a4738b642443c2535d347aa53ee2b7b80c800142c4844d94578769118ebfbc.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊂A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12399"] :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{(x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 : x^2+y^2 -1<0\\}\n$$\n\nThe boundary of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\partial A = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,: \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}} \\, \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $\\partial A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/8d3c7549948f0dd0f6a634526f1d978ae69d0c5b25611981b63a7cf389608087.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, the boundary points of $A$ lie on the circle $x^2+y^2 = 1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\partial A = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{x^2+y^2 = 1} \\, \\big\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-11035"] :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\,  y^2 - 1 \\leq  x , \\, -2 \\leq y \\leq  2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}, \\, -2 <     y <     2\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $\\partial A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nThe set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/1ca3afd22a68f2ce8b29645879967c3d130d5c19a7d529296ff129eb92693295.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = A - \\partial A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/6e7f7a326a89c60bbc11e48d491ed05a176161881368f189aec7aad916752b64.png)\n\nSo, in this case, the interior of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, \\bbox[5px, border: 1px solid black]{y^2 - 1 < x}, \\, -2 <     y <     2\\big\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-11034"] :question/problem "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ (x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\,:\\, x^2+y^2 \\geq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A - \\partial A = \\textrm{int}\\, A$\n\n- $\\textrm{int}\\, A \\cap \\partial A = \\emptyset$\n\n- $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A$" :question/worked-solution "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $\\partial A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/2c7e7ac1b320591250b045ebe493b28e0bb9cdb38469324ee496aacb8766c75b.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\textrm{int}\\, A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{int}\\,A = A - \\partial A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041552/assets/49ffc47c2ae1cde5e93e8607db8ef6b58eb7938913f82ea41f281df1fe334c14.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A - \\partial A = \\textrm{int}\\, A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\textrm{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\textrm{int}\\, A \\cap \\partial A = \\emptyset,$ where $\\emptyset$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement III is true. In this case, the set $A$ contains all of its boundary points, so we must have $A = \\textrm{int}\\,A \\cup \\partial A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
